`customerPrefix` is concatenated directly into the SQL text. A caller can pass `' OR 1=1; DROP TABLE orders; --` and run arbitrary SQL. The repository already uses parameters for the same table (FindByCustomerAsync).

Suggestion: cmd.CommandText = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE customer LIKE @prefix";
var p = cmd.CreateParameter(); p.ParameterName = "@prefix"; p.Value = EscapeLike(customerPrefix) + "%"; cmd.Parameters.Add(p);

`i <= start + size` returns size+1 items. A negative start also throws ArgumentOutOfRange instead of being validated.

Suggestion: ArgumentOutOfRangeException.ThrowIfNegative(start);
for (var i = start; i < start + size && i < items.Count; i++)

`customerPrefix` is concatenated directly into the SQL text, so a caller can inject arbitrary SQL. Pass the value as a `DbParameter` instead of building it into the query string.

Improved code:
cmd.CommandText = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE customer LIKE @prefix";
var prefixParam = cmd.CreateParameter();
prefixParam.ParameterName = "@prefix";
prefixParam.Value = customerPrefix + "%";
cmd.Parameters.Add(prefixParam);

The reset token is an MD5 hash of the email plus the current date, so anyone who knows a user's email can compute a valid token and take over the account. Generate the token from a cryptographically secure random source. Store it server-side with an expiry.

Improved code:
public static string ResetToken(string email)
{
    ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrEmpty(email);
    return Convert.ToHexString(RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(32));
}

