If no order matches `orderId`, `FirstOrDefault` returns null (Order is built with `new Order(...)`, so it is a reference type). `order.Total` then throws NullReferenceException for any unknown or mismatched order id. If Order is a struct, the method silently returns 0 instead, which is also wrong.

Suggestion: var order = found.FirstOrDefault(o => o.Id == orderId);
if (order is null) throw new KeyNotFoundException($"Order {orderId} not found for customer.");
return order.Total;

Concatenating user input into the SQL string allows SQL injection. Use a parameterized query, and escape LIKE wildcards in the prefix if literal matching is intended.

Improved code:
cmd.CommandText = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE customer LIKE @prefix";
var p = cmd.CreateParameter();
p.ParameterName = "@prefix";
p.Value = customerPrefix + "%";
cmd.Parameters.Add(p);
var result = await cmd.ExecuteScalarAsync(ct);

The reset token is deterministic, derived from the email and the date, so anyone who knows an email address can compute it and take over the account. Generate a cryptographically random token, store only its hash with an expiry, and compare it on use.

Improved code:
var bytes = RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(32);
return Convert.ToHexString(bytes);

`async void` makes exceptions unobservable and crashes the process, and callers can't await completion. The reader is never disposed. `Parallel.ForEach` mutates a non-thread-safe static `Dictionary` concurrently, which corrupts data or throws. Return `Task`, dispose the reader, and guard the shared state with a lock.

Improved code:
private static readonly object Gate = new();

public async Task ImportAsync(string csvPath)
{
    using var reader = new StreamReader(csvPath);
    var lines = new List<string>();
    string? line;
    while ((line = await reader.ReadLineAsync()) != null)
    {
        lines.Add(line);
    }

    Parallel.ForEach(lines.Skip(1), row =>
    {
        var cols = row.Split(',');
        var customer = cols[1];
        var total = decimal.Parse(cols[2]) * rates.ToUsd(cols[3]);
        lock (Gate)
        {
            TotalsByCustomer[customer] = TotalsByCustomer.GetValueOrDefault(customer) + total;
        }
    });
}

If no order matches `orderId`, `FirstOrDefault` returns null (Order is built with `new Order(...)`, so it is a reference type). `order.Total` then throws NullReferenceException for any unknown or mismatched order id. If Order is a struct, the method silently returns 0 instead, which is also wrong.

Suggestion: var order = found.FirstOrDefault(o => o.Id == orderId);
if (order is null) throw new KeyNotFoundException($"Order {orderId} not found for customer.");
return order.Total;

`name` is concatenated into a shell command passed to `execSync`. A name like `x; rm -rf /` runs arbitrary commands.

Suggestion: import { execFileSync } from "node:child_process";
// validate name against /^[\w-]+$/ first
return execFileSync("pdf-render", ["--out", "/tmp/reports", `${name}.pdf`]);

`===` on the HMAC hex string leaks timing information. In addition, a missing WEBHOOK_SECRET falls back to an empty key, so anyone can forge valid signatures.

Suggestion: if (!SECRET) throw new Error("WEBHOOK_SECRET missing");
const a = Buffer.from(expected), b = Buffer.from(signature);
return a.length === b.length && timingSafeEqual(a, b);

`name` is concatenated into a shell command, so input like `x; rm -rf ~` runs arbitrary commands. Pass arguments with `execFileSync` so no shell is involved. Also restrict `name` to a safe character set to block option injection and path traversal.

Improved code:
import { execFileSync } from "node:child_process";

export function exportReport(name: string): Buffer {
  if (!/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$/.test(name)) {
    throw new Error("Invalid report name");
  }
  return execFileSync("pdf-render", ["--out", "/tmp/reports", `${name}.pdf`]);
}

Calling `eval` on a user-supplied `formula` lets callers execute arbitrary JavaScript. Allow only numeric literals, arithmetic operators, parentheses and whitespace. Then evaluate in an isolated `Function` with strict mode, or use a dedicated expression parser.

Improved code:
export function evaluate(formula: string): number {
  if (!/^[0-9+\-*/%().\s]+$/.test(formula)) {
    throw new Error("Invalid formula");
  }
  return Number(new Function(`"use strict"; return (${formula});`)());
}

A live payments API token is hardcoded in source, which exposes it to anyone with repository access. Load it from the environment and fail fast if it is missing. The leaked token should also be rotated.

Improved code:
const paymentsToken = process.env.PAYMENTS_API_TOKEN;
if (!paymentsToken) {
  throw new Error("PAYMENTS_API_TOKEN is not set");
}
export const PAYMENTS_API_TOKEN: string = paymentsToken;

