The plain branch dereferences `rightLine` unconditionally, so a nil `rightLine` causes a nil-pointer panic. The old code handled a nil `rightLine` by falling back to `leftLine`. Fall back to `leftLine` when `rightLine` is nil, to keep the earlier robustness.

Improved code:
if diffLineType == DiffLinePlain {
		// left and right are the same, no need to do line-level diff, can just pick any side
		// caller always uses the "right side" for this type
		var lineHTML template.HTML
		if rightLine != nil {
			lineHTML = diffSection.getLineContentForRender(rightLine.RightIdx, rightLine, fileLanguage, highlightedRightLines)
		} else if leftLine != nil {
			lineHTML = diffSection.getLineContentForRender(leftLine.LeftIdx, leftLine, fileLanguage, highlightedLeftLines)
		}
		return diffInlineWithUnicodeEscape(lineHTML, locale)
	}

The old `Or` explicitly tolerated a nil receiver. The new `Combine` dereferences both `st` and `other` unconditionally. Any caller that still declares `var status *charset.EscapeStatus`, or passes a nil status (for example from an error path in `EscapeControlHTML`), now hits a nil-pointer panic instead of working. The four call sites switched from `x = x.Or(y)` to `x.Combine(y)` without any visible change to how `x` is initialised, so the safety now depends entirely on code outside this diff.

Suggestion: func (st *EscapeStatus) Combine(other *EscapeStatus) {
	if st == nil || other == nil {
		return
	}
	st.Escaped = st.Escaped || other.Escaped
	st.HasAmbiguous = st.HasAmbiguous || other.HasAmbiguous
	st.HasInvisible = st.HasInvisible || other.HasInvisible
}
// and make sure every caller initialises with &charset.EscapeStatus{}

`$hasLeft`/`$hasRight` are now true only when the MIME type string is non-empty. Before, a side rendered whenever its blob existed. If the backend leaves the MIME type empty for an existing blob, that side is silently dropped:
- an unrecognized or failed sniff;
- a blob too large to sniff;
- a type that is not an image.

When this happens, the swipe/overlay tabs also disappear, so the user sees a one-sided or empty diff with no error. This ties visibility to a sniffing detail instead of to blob presence.

Suggestion: Expose explicit `HasLeftBlob`/`HasRightBlob` (or nullable blob info) on the diff file and use them for visibility. Use the MIME type only for `data-mime-*`.

