Blob existence is inferred from the MIME string. If a blob fetch fails, the code still runs `DetectContentType` on an empty buffer. That can return a non-empty type such as `text/plain`, so the template wrongly shows a side or tab for a missing blob. Use the actual blob pointers instead.

Improved code:
{{$hasLeft := Iif (ne $file.LeftBlob nil) true false}}
{{$hasRight := Iif (ne $file.RightBlob nil) true false}}

If `ids` is empty or cannot be parsed, `runnerIDs` is empty. `FindRunnerOptions{IDs: nil}` then probably applies no ID filter, so the query returns every runner. A bulk "delete" would then wipe all runners. Reject requests that have no IDs before running the query.

Improved code:
runnerIDs = ctx.FormStringInt64s("ids")
	} else {
		ctx.HTTPError(http.StatusForbidden, "bulk actions are admin-only")
		return
	}
	if len(runnerIDs) == 0 {
		ctx.HTTPError(http.StatusBadRequest, "no runners selected")
		return
	}

The hidden `ids` input is set only once at init, when no checkboxes are selected yet. Every submission therefore sends an empty `ids`, so the bulk action never targets the selected runners. Update `formRunnerIds.value` inside `refresh`, which runs on every checkbox change, and drop the one-time assignment at the end of the function.

Improved code:
const refresh = () => {
    const checked = Array.from(rowCheckboxes).filter((c) => c.checked);
    toggleElem(toolbar, checked.length > 0);
    for (const btn of actionButtons) {
      btn.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.runner-bulk-count')!.textContent = `(${checked.length})`;
    }
    selectAll.checked = checked.length > 0 && checked.length === rowCheckboxes.length;
    selectAll.indeterminate = checked.length > 0 && checked.length < rowCheckboxes.length;
    formRunnerIds.value = checked.map((c) => c.getAttribute('data-runner-id')!).join(',');
  };

`formRunnerIds.value = collectSelectedIds()` runs once during initialization, when no checkbox is checked. It is never recomputed on checkbox change, select-all, or form submit, so `ids` stays an empty string. Pressing Delete/Disable/Enable therefore posts `ids=` and ignores what the user ticked. Combined with the handler lacking an empty-ID guard, an empty `IDs` filter may match every runner (global, org and repo-level), so one click on 'delete' could wipe them all.

Suggestion: Update the hidden input inside `refresh()`, and also on form `submit`/`click` before the fetch runs:

const refresh = () => {
  const checked = ...;
  formRunnerIds.value = checked.map((c) => c.getAttribute('data-runner-id')!).join(',');
  ...
};

If `ids` is empty or unparsable, `runnerIDs` is nil and `FindRunnerOptions{IDs: nil}` is passed to `db.Find`. The usual Gitea pattern is to apply the `IN` condition only when `len(IDs) > 0`, so an empty list likely returns all runners. A delete/disable would then affect every runner, and the user is told it succeeded. Malformed or missing input should never widen the scope of a destructive operation.

Suggestion: After parsing, add:
if len(runnerIDs) == 0 {
	ctx.HTTPError(http.StatusBadRequest, "no runners selected")
	return
}

The hidden `ids` input is populated only once at init, when nothing is selected, so it is always empty. The bulk form therefore submits no IDs and the action does nothing. Update the input inside `refresh()` on every selection change.

Improved code:
const collectSelectedIds = () => {
  const ids: string[] = [];
  for (const cb of rowCheckboxes) {
    if (cb.checked) ids.push(cb.getAttribute('data-runner-id')!);
  }
  return ids.join(',');
};
const syncIds = () => {
  formRunnerIds.value = collectSelectedIds();
};
selectAll.addEventListener('change', syncIds);
for (const cb of rowCheckboxes) cb.addEventListener('change', syncIds);
syncIds();
refresh();

