`errors.Join(srvHalfCloser.CloseRead())` replaces `err` instead of adding to it. Any copy error or client `CloseWrite` error collected just before is lost. Pass `err` as the first argument. The same fix is needed for `srvHalfCloser.CloseWrite()` in the other goroutine.

Improved code:
if srvIsHalfCloser {
			err = errors.Join(err, srvHalfCloser.CloseRead())
		}

This line also overwrites the accumulated `err`, so errors from `io.Copy` and from `clientHalfCloser.CloseRead()` are dropped. Include `err` in the join.

Improved code:
if srvIsHalfCloser {
			err = errors.Join(err, srvHalfCloser.CloseWrite())
		}

Waiting for both copy goroutines is only safe when both connections support half-close. Otherwise, finishing one direction cannot unblock the other `io.Copy`, and the handler can hang forever, leaking goroutines and connections. Fall back to the old behavior of returning after the first result when either side is not a `HalfCloser`.

Improved code:
if !clientIsHalfCloser || !srvIsHalfCloser {
		// Cannot propagate half-close; return when either direction ends
		// so the deferred Close unblocks the other copy.
		return <-errc
	}
	// Wait for both sides of the connection to close.
	var errs []error
	for range 2 {
		errs = append(errs, <-errc)
	}
	return errors.Join(errs...)

In the backend-to-client goroutine, `err = errors.Join(srvHalfCloser.CloseRead())` overwrites `err` and drops the earlier copy error and the client `CloseWrite` error. Join it with `err` so those errors are preserved.

Improved code:
if clientIsHalfCloser {
			err = errors.Join(err, clientHalfCloser.CloseWrite())
		}
		if srvIsHalfCloser {
			err = errors.Join(err, srvHalfCloser.CloseRead())
		}
		errc <- err

If either side's connection doesn't support half-close (the no-op fallback), or the peer never closes its side, one copy may never finish after the other ends. Waiting for both goroutines can then hang the handler indefinitely, since `defer srv.Close()` only runs on return. When a direction ends without half-close support, fully close both connections so the other `io.Copy` unblocks.

Improved code:
// Wait for both sides of the connection to close.
	var errs []error
	for i := 0; i < 2; i++ {
		errs = append(errs, <-errc)
		if i == 0 && (!srvIsHalfCloser || !clientIsHalfCloser) {
			// Half-close isn't possible on both ends; tear down so the
			// other io.Copy doesn't block forever.
			srv.Close()
			c.clientConn.Close()
		}
	}
	return errors.Join(errs...)

When a 429/5xx response triggers a retry, the closure returns `(true, nil)` and has two problems. The response body is never closed, so each retry leaks a connection. And once retries run out, the pacer returns a nil error, so the caller goes on to use a failed 5xx response as if it succeeded. The PR widens this to all `retryErrorCodes`, so the fix is to close the body and return a non-nil error whenever a retry is requested.

Improved code:
res, err = client.Do(req)
		if err != nil {
			return shouldRetry(ctx, nil, err)
		}
		if fserrors.ShouldRetryHTTP(res, retryErrorCodes) {
			_ = res.Body.Close()
			return true, fmt.Errorf("download request failed with status %d", res.StatusCode)
		}
		return false, nil

`client.Do` returns a successful `res` with a 5xx/429 status. The closure then returns `true, nil`, so the pacer retries and overwrites `res` without closing the previous `res.Body`. Before this change only 429 triggered a retry; now every 5xx does, so the leak is far more likely during outages. Each leaked body holds a connection until GC.

Suggestion: if fserrors.ShouldRetryHTTP(res, retryErrorCodes) {
	fs.CheckClose(res.Body, &err)
	return true, fmt.Errorf("HTTP error %d", res.StatusCode)
}
return false, nil

When a retryable status (e.g. 429/503) is returned, the pacer retries but `res` and its `Body` are never closed, leaking connections on every retry. Drain and close the body before signalling a retry. Also return an error for the retry case so the final failure is surfaced rather than a nil error with a bad response.

Improved code:
res, err = client.Do(req)
if err != nil {
    return shouldRetry(ctx, nil, err)
}
if fserrors.ShouldRetryHTTP(res, retryErrorCodes) {
    _, _ = io.Copy(io.Discard, res.Body)
    _ = res.Body.Close()
    return true, fmt.Errorf("retryable status: %d", res.StatusCode)
}
return false, nil

