    return i == url.Length ? "/" : string.Concat("/", url.AsSpan(i));
}

The normalizer only handles targets that start with `/`. A target starting with `\` (e.g. `\example.com`) or `\/example.com` is returned unchanged. The callers then prepend `/` because `pattern[0] != '/'`, which turns it into `/\example.com`. Browsers treat that as scheme-relative, so the open redirect remains. Treat a leading `\` like a leading `/` and collapse the whole run.

Improved code:
if (url.Length == 0 || (url[0] != '/' && url[0] != '\\'))
{
    return url;
}

var i = 1;
while (i < url.Length && (url[i] == '/' || url[i] == '\\'))
{
    i++;
}

// A single leading '/' is already safe.
if (i == 1 && url[0] == '/')
{
    return url;
}

return i == url.Length ? "/" : string.Concat("/", url.AsSpan(i));

The new "anchor at end" shortcut compares the viewport end against `Bounds`. `Bounds` holds the size from the previous arrange pass. Before the first arrange, `Bounds` is still zero, so any non-zero viewport start satisfies the check. The panel then jumps to the last item instead of estimating the correct anchor. Only apply this shortcut when the panel has a valid, non-zero arranged size.

Improved code:
var horizontal = Orientation == Orientation.Horizontal;
var viewportEnd = horizontal ? _viewport.Right : _viewport.Bottom;
var boundsU = horizontal ? Bounds.Width : Bounds.Height;

if (!_hasReachedEnd &&
    boundsU > 0 &&
    MathUtilities.GreaterThanOrClose(viewportEnd, boundsU))
{
    index = itemCount - 1;
    position = viewportEnd - EstimateElementSizeU();
    return;
}

This condition must stay identical to the one in `GetOrEstimateAnchorElementForViewport`. Otherwise the realization step may re-position an anchor that was not chosen by the end shortcut. For example, the last item may be picked through normal estimation while `Bounds` is zero. Apply the same non-zero bounds guard here so the two checks stay consistent.

Improved code:
var viewportEnd = horizontal ? _viewport.Right : _viewport.Bottom;
var boundsU = horizontal ? Bounds.Width : Bounds.Height;
var anchorAtEnd = !_hasReachedEnd && index == items.Count - 1 &&
    boundsU > 0 &&
    MathUtilities.GreaterThanOrClose(viewportEnd, boundsU);

When `Bounds` is still empty (for example, an offset was applied before the first arrange), `viewportEnd >= 0` is always true. This wrongly jumps the anchor to the last item. Require a non-zero panel extent before using the "at end" shortcut. Apply the same guard to `anchorAtEnd` in `RealizeElements` so both checks agree.

Improved code:
var boundsU = horizontal ? Bounds.Width : Bounds.Height;

if (!_hasReachedEnd &&
    boundsU > 0 &&
    MathUtilities.GreaterThanOrClose(viewportEnd, boundsU))
{
    index = itemCount - 1;
    position = viewportEnd - EstimateElementSizeU();
    return;
}

The new guard also discards repositions caused by `_childMargin` changes, not only by size changes. When the margin changes, such as when the shadow or decoration margin is updated, the popup is no longer shifted to compensate, so its content is visually offset from the pointer anchor. Suppress only the size-triggered reposition and keep margin-triggered updates.

